  "grammar": {
    "title": "Subject Pronouns and Possessive Adjectives",
    "explanation": "We use **subject pronouns** instead of names. **I, you, he, she, we, they** are subject pronouns.\n\n**I** means me. **You** means the person I talk to.\n\n**He** is one boy. **She** is one girl.\n\n**We** means you and me together. **They** means two or more other people.\n\nWe use **possessive adjectives** to show something is mine. **My, your, his, her, our, their** are possessive adjectives.\n\nThe possessive adjective stands **before the noun**. I say **my cat**, not cat my.\n\n**My** is for me. **Your** is for you. **His** is for one boy and **her** is for one girl.\n\n**Our** is for we. **Their** is for they.\n\nA common mistake: say **I am happy**, not me am happy. Say **This is my book**, not this is me book.",
    "examples": [
      "*I* have one cat.",
      "*She* has two dogs.",
      "*He* has three pens. *They* are blue.",
      "*I* see five birds. *They* are in the sky.",
      "This is *my* book.",
      "*We* love *our* teacher."
    ],
